      "source": [
        "In Project 2 and 3, I built a spline based linear model and regularized it with Ridge and Lasso. For Project 4, I kept those models as my benchmarks and tested whether a more advanced ensemble could beat them. I added the categorical housing variables back through one-hot encoding, since my linear models had excluded them. I then tuned a Random Forest and a Gradient Boosting model using the same training only cross validation process.\n",
        "\n",
        "In cross validation, Gradient Boosting reached an RMSE of 24,832 and Random Forest reached 26,331. My best Project 2 or 3 model, Lasso, scored 34,040. Gradient Boosting won, so I scored only that model on the held out test set, once. It reached an RMSE of 24,578, a MAE of 14,717, and an R squared of 0.925. My best benchmark on the same test set, the unregularized spline model, scored an RMSE of 36,092, a MAE of 22,406, and an R squared of 0.838. That is about a 32% reduction in RMSE and a 34% reduction in MAE, and the model now explains about 9 more percentage points of the variation in sale price."
